**BI-RADS** (Breast Imaging-Reporting and Data System) is a standardized system created by the American College of Radiology to ensure consistent reporting of breast imaging studies (mammography, ultrasound, MRI). Each category indicates the likelihood of malignancy and recommended clinical action:

| Category | Meaning | Cancer Risk | Recommended Action |
|----------|---------|-------------|-------------------|
| **0** | Incomplete | N/A | Additional imaging or comparison with prior exams needed |
| **1** | Negative | ~0% | No findings; continue routine screening |
| **2** | Benign | ~0% | Non-cancerous findings; routine screening |
| **3** | Probably Benign | <2% | Short-interval follow-up (usually 6 months) |
| **4** | Suspicious | 2–95% | Biopsy recommended (subdivided: 4A low, 4B moderate, 4C high suspicion) |
| **5** | Highly Suggestive of Malignancy | >95% | Prompt biopsy and appropriate treatment |
| **6** | Known Biopsy-Proven Malignancy | 100% | Used for pre-treatment staging or monitoring known cancer |

**Key Notes:**
- BI-RADS applies to imaging findings, not clinical diagnosis.
- Categories guide next steps but don't replace provider discussion.
- Always review your report with your healthcare team for context-specific advice.

